\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity, yet the causal relationship between ambiguity type and safety violations remains unmeasured. We present a matched-pair study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluated across five architecturally diverse models (2,997 episodes). Ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 percentage points (OR $= 15.1$), with a perturbation control supporting ambiguity-specific causation. A significant type hierarchy emerges (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$), with a robust two-tier structure among five types and incompleteness as an upper bound partly reflecting an information-addition confound. The hierarchy replicates on two additional model families (LLaMA $\rho = 0.90$, $p = 0.037$; Gemini $\rho = 0.60$, directionally consistent): incompleteness is consistently high (46--62\% across models), while authorization scope varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4, though authorization scope carries the widest measurement uncertainty among types), suggesting that safety training may differentially mitigate specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness, with over 88\% surface adoption. Our Differential Nocuity framework connects type-specific vulnerability to disambiguation channel availability and suggests both type-aware policy auditing and externalized ambiguity detection for policy-governed agent deployments.
\end{abstract}

