\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type hierarchy (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). Of 1,497 matched pairs, the discordant ratio is 15.1:1 (544 ambiguous-only vs.\ 36 unambiguous-only violations). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp, CI containing zero), and results replicate under an alternative severity threshold (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Clause identity explains 22.4\% of variance versus 1.0\% for model identity. A replication on two additional model families (Gemini-2.5-Flash, LLaMA-4-Scout; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms the binary effect across seven models spanning six providers ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp, both $p < 10^{-6}$).

\subsection{Type Hierarchy (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model. $\Delta$pp denotes the pooled difference between ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across all five models. All per-type differences are significant ($p < 10^{-5}$, Fisher's exact test).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrrr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & $\Delta$pp & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness & 53.6 & 46.0 & 58.0 & 48.0 & 62.0 & 54.0 & +49.2 & $<10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope & 41.6 & 64.0 & 46.0 & 12.0 & 26.0 & 60.0 & +36.0 & $<10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Precedence & 36.8 & 34.0 & 36.0 & 36.0 & 40.0 & 38.0 & +30.8 & $<10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42.0 & 34.0 & 32.0 & 52.0 & 54.0 & +35.2 & $<10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42.0 & 38.0 & 32.0 & 36.7 & 48.0 & +34.2 & $<10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18.0 & 32.0 & 22.0 & 42.0 & 44.0 & +18.7 & $<10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41.0 & 40.7 & 30.3 & 43.1 & 49.7 & \textbf{+34.0} & $<10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$; statistical power $= 0.993$ at the observed effect). TOST equivalence testing at a $\pm 15$ pp margin reveals a two-tier structure among five types---authorization scope (41.6\%), lexical (42.8\%), scopal (39.4\%), and conditional precedence (36.8\%) form a mid-tier cluster, with coreferential (31.6\%) below---while incompleteness (53.6\%) stands apart as an upper bound whose ranking partly reflects an information-addition confound (\S\ref{sec:results}, Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Sensitivity analysis across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$ pp; Appendix~\ref{app:tost}) confirms this structure: the two-tier pattern emerges at $\pm 12$ pp (5/6 mid-tier pairs equivalent) and stabilizes at $\pm 15$ pp (6/6).

Incompleteness produces the highest violation rate in four of five models (all except GPT-5.4, where authorization scope ranks first at 64\%), with rates $\geq$46\% confirmed in all seven models tested (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}). Incompleteness is structurally distinct: its unambiguous variant adds policy content rather than merely resolving ambiguity, so its 49.2 pp effect is an upper bound; a confound analysis confirms the effect remains significant after controlling for information delta (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). This pattern replicates on two additional model families (Gemini 48\%, LLaMA 52\%; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}), further suggesting that incompleteness severity reflects a genuine property of the ambiguity type rather than a dataset artifact. Coreferential, whose variants are lexically identical, provides a confound-free lower bound at 31.6\%. Excluding incompleteness, pairwise contrasts show authorization scope and lexical remain significantly above coreferential (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp, ambiguous minus unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. All types produce significant increases. A two-tier structure emerges among five types (mid cluster $\gg$ coreferential), with incompleteness as an upper bound partly reflecting an information-addition confound.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: it does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, and controlling for token delta reverses the direction. Cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52 pp across models (Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), while incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure*}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), consistent with safety training that addresses boundary reasoning; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope pattern (Claude lowest, DeepSeek highest) is robust under both severity thresholds and within-judge analysis (Claude 12\%, Qwen3 26\%, DeepSeek 60\%, all judged by GPT-5.4). This within-judge comparison controls for judge identity, confirming that model differentiation on authorization scope is not an artifact of cross-judge assignment. We note that authorization scope has the lowest per-type judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}), so absolute rates carry more uncertainty than for other types, though the 48 pp within-judge range far exceeds the measurement noise.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers (linguistic and specification, \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal \emph{Differential Nocuity}: all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, OR $= 15.1$), but the type primarily determines severity, with a smaller but significant model$\times$type interaction (2.9\% of variance). Incompleteness is consistently high (46--62\% violation rate across all models), while authorization scope vulnerability varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4), suggesting that safety training may differentially mitigate specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness across all types, adopting surface interpretations in over 88\% of cases.

Our contributions are: (1)~the first causal quantification of policy ambiguity's safety impact via matched-pair design with perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:method}), replicated across seven models; (2)~a Differential Nocuity framework revealing a robust two-tier type hierarchy ($V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$), with incompleteness as a confounded upper bound and \emph{[exploratory]} authorization scope as a potential canary type for safety training (\S\ref{sec:results}); and (3)~a failure mode taxonomy with the empirical phenomenon of \emph{Ambiguity Blindness} ($<$11\% awareness; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).

